{#- Single source of the GALEC-derived C symbol policy and of the symbol
    tables every C artifact prints from.

    Registered in the shared render environment as `galec-c-symbols.jinja`
    (like `galec-model.c.jinja`), so the header, the source, and the eFMI
    Production Code manifest all import THIS file:

        {%- import "galec-c-symbols.jinja" as c_symbol_policy -%}
        {%- set c_symbols = c_symbol_policy.symbols -%}
        {%- set c_state_symbols = c_symbol_policy.state_symbols -%}

    Importing shares one allocation instead of recreating it from copied
    inputs: `allocate_symbols` is pure and deterministic, so a single policy
    declaration and a single pair of allocation calls give every artifact the
    same checked mapping by construction. A divergent copy would rename the
    same variable differently across artifacts (a header field the manifest's
    LogicalData no longer names, for example), which is exactly the failure a
    second declaration site reintroduces. No template may declare its own
    reserved list.

    The policy lives here, in the target directory, rather than in
    `rumoca-phase-codegen` Rust: SPEC_0029 §12 keeps target-specific constants
    and textual target policy in the owning target's `target.toml` and
    templates, and SPEC_0034 D2 leaves Rust with the collision-checked
    semantic context (`algorithm_code.symbol_names` /
    `algorithm_code.variable_names`) and the generic allocation command.

    `reserved` holds every fixed identifier these templates emit that the
    allocator cannot infer: C keywords, the `rumoca_galec_` helpers, and the
    <math.h>/<stdint.h> spellings the C body writes verbatim. Identifiers the
    templates never emit (fminf/fmaxf/fmodf: Real min/max are the relational
    `rumoca_galec_` helpers) stay out, and generated names carrying the
    `rumoca_galec_` prefix are rejected by the allocator's prefix rule. -#}
{%- set reserved = [
    "auto", "break", "case", "char", "const", "continue", "default", "do",
    "double", "else", "enum", "extern", "float", "for", "goto", "if",
    "inline", "int", "long", "register", "restrict", "return", "short",
    "signed", "sizeof", "static", "struct", "switch", "typedef", "union",
    "unsigned", "void", "volatile", "while",
    "_Alignas", "_Alignof", "_Atomic", "_BitInt", "_Bool", "_Complex",
    "_Decimal32", "_Decimal64", "_Decimal128", "_Generic", "_Imaginary",
    "_Noreturn", "_Static_assert", "_Thread_local",
    "alignas", "alignof", "bool", "constexpr", "false", "nullptr",
    "static_assert", "thread_local", "true", "typeof", "typeof_unqual",
    "self", "rumoca_galec_sign", "rumoca_galec_min", "rumoca_galec_max",
    "rumoca_galec_bounded_index",
    "rumoca_galec_imin", "rumoca_galec_imax", "rumoca_galec_division_towards_zero",
    "rumoca_galec_compare_lt", "rumoca_galec_compare_gt", "rumoca_galec_compare_le",
    "rumoca_galec_compare_ge", "rumoca_galec_compare_eq", "rumoca_galec_compare_ne",
    "rumoca_galec_error_signal_status",
    "int32_t", "uint32_t", "INT32_C", "UINT32_C", "isnan",
    "fabsf", "sqrtf", "expf", "logf", "log10f", "floorf", "ceilf",
    "sinf", "cosf", "tanf", "asinf", "acosf", "atanf", "atan2f",
    "sinhf", "coshf", "tanhf", "powf"
] -%}
{#- The identifiers the `rumoca_galec_` helper bodies in `model.c.jinja`
    declare themselves: `value`/`extent` (bounded_index and sign),
    `u1`/`u2` (the Real min/max helpers), `status`/`lhs`/`rhs` (the six
    comparison helpers, imin/imax, division_towards_zero) and `result` (every
    one of them). They are the only identifiers these templates emit WITHOUT
    the generated prefix, so the allocator's prefix rule does not cover them,
    and a file-scope symbol of the same name — a GALEC function named `result`
    becomes `static void result(...)` — is shadowed by the helper local of
    that name under the profile's -Wshadow -Werror. Keep this list in step
    with the helper bodies.

    It is added to the `symbols` table only. That table is the one that can
    yield a file-scope C identifier (the user function names), and it cannot
    tell those apart from the locals and parameters it also holds, so it takes
    the conservative whole list. `state_symbols` spells struct members, which
    live in their own C namespace, shadow nothing, and are only ever read as
    `self-><member>`; reserving there would rename fields (and the manifest
    components naming them) for no gain.

    The private working-memory aggregate needs nothing in `reserved`. It is one
    file-scope object spelled `rumoca_galec_scratch`, holding one union per
    overlay group spelled `rumoca_galec_g<n>`; the prefix rule already makes
    every one of those unallocatable, and listing them would additionally rename
    every source symbol spelled `scratch` — the allocator rejects a candidate
    whose prefixed spelling is reserved, so the entries would buy nothing and
    cost a rename.

    Four entries do belong here. `ctx` is the per-owner region alias every generated
    function and method declares at its top, in the same block scope as the
    frame locals — a GALEC local spelled `ctx` would be a redeclaration, not a
    shadow. `startup`/`recalibrate`/`dostep` name the three method regions
    inside that aggregate, where they sit beside one member per user function;
    reserving them keeps a GALEC function of the same name from claiming a
    member that is already spoken for. All four are withheld from
    `state_symbols` for the same reason as the helper locals: they never
    compete with a declared block variable. -#}
{%- set helper_locals = [
    "result", "value", "extent", "lhs", "rhs", "u1", "u2", "status",
    "ctx", "startup", "recalibrate", "dostep"
] -%}
{#- `state_policy` is the base declaration — the separator and the generated
    namespace are spelled once, here — and `policy` is that same declaration
    with the helper locals withheld. Derived rather than copied: two literal
    namespace declarations in one file is the same divergence hazard as two
    declarations in two files. -#}
{%- set state_policy = {
    "separator": "_",
    "reserved": reserved,
    "generated_prefixes": ["rumoca_galec_"]
} -%}
{%- set policy = {
    "separator": state_policy.separator,
    "reserved": reserved + helper_locals,
    "generated_prefixes": state_policy.generated_prefixes
} -%}
{#- `symbols` maps every source reference the block body may print (block
    name, locals, parameters, functions, fields); `state_symbols` maps the
    declared block variables that become state-struct fields and manifest
    components. Both tables are allocated from the one reserved list above —
    the file-scope-capable one also withholding the helper locals — under the
    one generated namespace. -#}
{%- set symbols = allocate_symbols(algorithm_code.symbol_names, policy) -%}
{%- set state_symbols = allocate_symbols(algorithm_code.variable_names, state_policy) -%}
